{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A small research team should build a custom experiment tracker only when the experimental design requires the tracker to function as an active controller in a closed-loop system (e.g., active learning or Bayesian optimization), where the latency and schema rigidity of existing tools would break the causal chain of the experiment; otherwise, they must adopt an existing tool to avoid the 'integration tax' and 'migration cliff'.","counterconditions":["The team lacks the engineering bandwidth to maintain a custom state machine without halting research progress.","The active learning loop can be simulated via external scripts that interface with an existing tracker's API without requiring the tracker itself to be the controller.","The experimental schema is stable enough that a rigid existing tool does not force data distortion."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"f0a7fdb1573a11c5f3064989fd549eaaaafda316476c85270e892ed4058bef0b","quote":"Build a custom tracker only if the experiment involves active learning loops where the tracker must act as the controller"},{"block":"b1642d037422b51c0be00c872d50beb7dbcd5a47b2733f33a65b653bf158ecb0","quote":"enforcing a 'mechanist' causal model of experimentation"}],"mechanism":"Causal Control Boundary: The decision boundary is defined by whether the tracker must write back to the experimental apparatus in real-time. If the tracker is purely observational (read-only log), existing tools maximize bandwidth. If the tracker is actuatory (read-write control loop), custom software is required to maintain the causal mechanism's integrity, as off-the-shelf tools abstract away the specific state-transition logic needed for novel active learning loops.","optional_refs":["b1642d037422b51c0be00c872d50beb7dbcd5a47b2733f33a65b653bf158ecb0","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["b1642d037422b51c0be00c872d50beb7dbcd5a47b2733f33a65